.TH BCRAFT 1 "September 2026" "bcraft 0.4.0" "Basecraft Linux"
.SH NAME
bcraft \- small build and binary package tool for Basecraft Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bcraft
.B build
.I package.src.tar.gz
.PP
.B bcraft
.B install
.I package.tar.gz
.PP
.B bcraft
.B update
.I package.tar.gz
.PP
.B bcraft
.B remove
.I package
.PP
.B bcraft
.B list
.PP
.B bcraft
.B info
.I package
.PP
.B bcraft
.BR -v " | " --version
.PP
.B bcraft
.BR -h " | " --help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bcraft
is a deliberately small build and package management tool for Basecraft Linux.
It is designed around reproducible package recipes, staged installation into a
package directory, binary tar.gz packages, manifests, and a small local package
database.
.PP
bcraft does not perform automatic dependency solving or repository transactions.
Package recipes are expected to describe how software is built and installed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "build " package.src.tar.gz
Build a source recipe. Archive sources are downloaded and verified, the recipe
is executed, files are staged in PKGDIR, a manifest is generated, and a binary
package is written below /var/cache/basecraft/packages.
.TP
.BI "install " package.tar.gz
Install a binary package and register its PKGINFO and MANIFEST below
/var/lib/basecraft/packages.
.TP
.BI "update " package.tar.gz
Update an already installed package. Files present in both package versions are
replaced, obsolete package files are removed, and package metadata is updated.
Configuration files receive special handling as described below.
.TP
.BI "remove " package
Remove files belonging to an installed package. Shared non-empty directories are
left in place.
.TP
.B list
List installed packages and their versions.
.TP
.BI "info " package
Display PKGINFO for an installed package.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --version
Display the bcraft version.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display command usage.
.SH SOURCE RECIPES
A recipe normally contains package.conf and an executable build.sh.
.PP
Archive based packages use source_mode="archive" together with source_url,
source_file and source_sha256.
.PP
Packages whose source is contained directly in the recipe may use
source_mode="recipe".
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
Regular files below /etc are automatically treated as configuration files.
Additional configuration files outside /etc can be declared in package.conf:
.PP
.nf
config_files=(
    "opt/example/settings.conf"
    "usr/share/example/local.conf"
)
.fi
.PP
Paths are relative to the filesystem root.
.PP
For configuration files bcraft stores the SHA-256 hash of the package version
in the package manifest. During update, if the installed file still matches the
old package hash, it is replaced normally. If the local file was modified, the
local file is preserved and the new package version is written as:
.PP
.nf
/path/to/file.bcraft-new
.fi
.PP
When an older bcraft manifest does not contain configuration hashes, bcraft
uses the conservative behavior and preserves the existing configuration while
writing the new package version as .bcraft-new.
.PP
During removal, an unchanged configuration file is removed. A modified
configuration file is preserved.
.SH MANIFEST
Current manifests use three tab-separated fields:
.PP
.nf
TYPE    SHA256-or--    PATH
.fi
.PP
Types are:
.TP
.B f
regular package file
.TP
.B c
configuration file
.TP
.B l
symbolic link
.TP
.B d
directory
.PP
bcraft can also read the older two-field manifest format used by bcraft 0.2.x.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/basecraft-release
Basecraft base identifier used for package compatibility checks.
.TP
.I /var/lib/basecraft/build
Package build trees.
.TP
.I /var/lib/basecraft/packages
Installed package database.
.TP
.I /var/cache/basecraft/sources
Downloaded source archive cache.
.TP
.I /var/cache/basecraft/packages
Built binary package cache.
.SH NOTES
Packages with base=any do not require /etc/basecraft-release. This is useful for
architecture-independent bootstrap tools such as bcraft itself.
.PP
Home directory configuration such as ~/.config is not managed by bcraft unless
a package explicitly installs such files into the package payload, which normal
packages should not do.
